diagnosis_text:
The network is fully operational. No fault was found at the time of
investigation. The eBGP session between router1 (AS 1) and router2 (AS 2)
over the 193.10.11.0/24 inter-router link (eth0 on both sides) is
established and functioning correctly: router1 receives the 200.1.1.0/24
prefix via BGP and router2 receives the 195.11.14.0/24 prefix via BGP,
giving both routers complete forwarding state. Both local (eth1) interfaces
are up on each router, and a live ping from pc1 to pc2 (200.1.1.2) returned
4/4 packets with zero loss and ~1 ms round-trip time. The symptom reported
in ticket #9011 — "pc1 cannot reach pc2" — is not reproducible in the
current state of the lab. The operations team's hypothesis that "the cable
between the two buildings went down" was incorrect; the inter-router link is
demonstrably up and carrying BGP traffic right now. The most likely
explanation is that the problem was transient (e.g., a brief BGP session
hiccup or a momentary link glitch that self-recovered before this
investigation began) and has since resolved on its own. No corrective
action is required. If the symptom recurs, monitoring BGP session uptime
(`show bgp summary`) and interface error counters (`ip -s link`) on both
routers would help determine whether the flap is link-layer or BGP-timer
driven.
